        "::: {.callout-note appearance=\"minimal\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Abstract\n",
        "\n",
        "**EPIDEMIOLOGY OF HOSPITALIZATIONS FOR ENDOCRINE AND METABOLIC DISEASES IN CHILE: TRENDS AND SPATIAL AUTOCORRELATION, 2010-2024**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Introduction:** Endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (ICD-10 E00-E89) represent a growing burden on health systems. In Chile, diabetes mellitus prevalence reaches 12.3% and obesity 34.4% among adults.\n",
        "\n",
        " **Materials and Methods:** Descriptive ecological study using the Hospital Discharge Database (EGRESOS) from DEIS, Chilean Ministry of Health, 2010–2024. Discharges with primary diagnosis E00–E89 were classified into 10 diagnostic categories. Age-standardized rates (ASR) were computed using the WHO World Standard Population. Municipal-level geospatial analysis employed Moran's I and LISA with Queen contiguity weights. \n",
        " \n",
        " **Results:** A total of 616,564 hospital discharges were analyzed (annual mean: 41,104). The overall ASR increased from 165.61 (2010) to 272.16 per 100,000 (2024), representing a 64.3% rise, with a transient pandemic-related decline (143.44 in 2020). Females predominated (M:F ratio = 0.59; female ASR 356.65 vs. 190.78 for males, 2024). Obesity (E65-E68) accounted for 32.2% of discharges (198,410), followed by diabetes (E08-E13, 28.1%; 173,132) and metabolic disorders (E70-E88, 20.1%; 123,838). Aysén (298.08) and Magallanes (263.30) had the highest crude rates; Coquimbo the lowest (125.24). Global Moran's I confirmed significant clustering (I = 0.20; p < 0.001), with obesity showing the strongest autocorrelation (I = 0.61). LISA identified 23 hot spots and 22 cold spots for E00-E89, and 54 hot spots for obesity. \n",
        " \n",
        " **Conclusions:** Endocrine hospitalizations in Chile show accelerated growth driven by obesity and diabetes, with female predominance and significant geospatial clustering, providing evidence for targeted interventions.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Keywords:** metabolic diseases; diabetes mellitus; obesity; age-standardized rates; spatial analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
