        "\n",
        "# Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "Endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (ICD-10 codes E00-E89) constitute a heterogeneous group of pathologies encompassing thyroid disorders (E00-E07), diabetes mellitus (E08-E13), other glucose regulation disorders (E15-E16), disorders of other endocrine glands (E20-E35), intraoperative endocrine complications (E36), malnutrition (E40-E46), other nutritional deficiencies (E50-E64), obesity and overweight (E65-E68), metabolic disorders (E70-E88), and post-surgical endocrine complications (E89). Collectively, these conditions represent one of the leading causes of morbidity, disability, and mortality globally, and their prevalence has increased steadily over recent decades as a consequence of epidemiological transition, urbanization, population aging, and dietary pattern changes [@gbd2023diabetes; @ncdrisc2024trends].\n",
        "\n",
        "Globally, diabetes mellitus affects approximately 537 million adults, a figure projected to exceed 783 million by 2045, with a disproportionate burden in middle-income countries [@idf2021atlas]. Obesity has reached epidemic proportions, with over 1 billion people with obesity worldwide in 2022, and is causally associated with type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, certain cancers, and premature mortality [@lancet2024obesity; @ncdrisc2024trends]. Thyroid disorders, particularly hypothyroidism and thyrotoxicosis, affect 5–10% of the adult population in most countries and constitute a common cause of hospitalization when presenting with acute complications such as thyrotoxic crisis or myxedema coma [@taylor2018epidemiology]. Malnutrition, in both its protein-calorie form and specific micronutrient deficiencies, remains a public health problem in vulnerable populations, including hospitalized elderly adults, where prevalence ranges from 20–50% depending on the screening tool used [@cederholm2017espen]. Inborn errors of metabolism, though individually rare, collectively affect approximately 1 in 800 live births and frequently require recurrent hospitalizations [@saudubray2018overview].\n",
        "\n",
        "In Chile, these pathologies hold particular epidemiological relevance. The 2016–2017 National Health Survey reported a diabetes mellitus prevalence of 12.3% in the adult population, with an additional 3.2% of previously undiagnosed cases [@minsal2018ens]. The prevalence of obesity (BMI ≥ 30) reached 34.4%, and overweight (BMI 25–29.9) reached 39.8%, placing Chile among countries with the highest rates of excess weight in Latin America [@minsal2018ens; @oecd2023health]. Thyroid disorders are particularly common, with hypothyroidism prevalence estimated at 18.6% in women over 60 years [@fardella2001prevalencia]. Hospital malnutrition in Chile has been documented with prevalences of 30–47% in hospitalized adult patients [@correia2017hospital]. This convergence of high prevalence of chronic metabolic diseases and persistent malnutrition in vulnerable populations generates a \"double burden\" of malnutrition that directly reflects on demand for hospital services.\n",
        "\n",
        "Hospital discharge records (EGRESOS) from the Department of Statistics and Health Information (DEIS) of Chile's Ministry of Health constitute an administrative data source that captures virtually all publicly funded hospitalization episodes in the country, covering both the public sector (FONASA) and, partially, the private sector (ISAPRE). Although these records reflect hospitalizations rather than incident diagnoses per se, they constitute a robust indicator of disease burden and health service utilization at the population level [@minsal2024egresos]. The continuous availability of EGRESOS data since 2001, combined with census-based population projections from the National Institute of Statistics (INE), enables the calculation of age-standardized hospitalization rates comparable across regions, time periods, and demographic subgroups.\n",
        "\n",
        "This analysis covers the period 2010–2024 and has the following objectives: (1) calculate age-standardized hospitalization rates (ASR) overall and by disease category within the E00-E89 spectrum; (2) describe temporal trends over 15 years; (3) identify disparities by sex, age group, and administrative region; (4) evaluate geospatial clustering patterns at the municipal level using spatial autocorrelation statistics; and (5) provide a detailed epidemiological profile for each of the 10 individual ICD-10 categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Methods\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data Sources\n",
        "\n",
        "Two main data sources were used. The first is the Hospital Discharge Database (EGRESOS) maintained by the Department of Statistics and Health Information (DEIS) of Chile's Ministry of Health, covering fiscal years 2010 to 2024. Each record contains the primary discharge diagnosis coded according to the International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision (ICD-10), along with patient demographic information (sex, age group), geographic identifiers (region and municipality of residence), and administrative variables (discharge year). EGRESOS captures all discharges from public hospitals of the National Health Services System (SNSS) and, since 2012, progressively includes discharges from private institutions, providing near-universal coverage of hospitalization episodes in Chile.\n",
        "\n",
        "The second source is the population projections database produced by the National Institute of Statistics (INE) based on the 2017 National Census. These projections provide annual population estimates disaggregated by region, municipality, individual age, and sex for the period 2002–2035. The projections were stored in Apache Parquet format and accessed programmatically.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Case Selection\n",
        "\n",
        "All hospital discharges with a primary diagnosis (DIAG1 field) corresponding to ICD-10 codes E00 to E89 (endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases) were included, encompassing all subcodes (e.g., E10.0 to E10.9). Records with missing or invalid diagnosis codes, missing sex, or missing age group were excluded. The 10 categories comprise: thyroid disorders (E00-E07), diabetes mellitus (E08-E13), other glucose regulation disorders (E15-E16), disorders of other endocrine glands (E20-E35), intraoperative complications (E36), malnutrition (E40-E46), other nutritional deficiencies (E50-E64), obesity and overweight (E65-E68), metabolic disorders (E70-E88), and post-surgical endocrine complications (E89).\n",
        "\n",
        "## Age Standardization\n",
        "\n",
        "Direct standardization was applied using the WHO World Standard Population [@ahmad2001age], which comprises 17 five-year age groups from 0–4 to 80+. The weights used are: 0–4: 8,860; 5–9: 8,690; 10–14: 8,600; 15–19: 8,470; 20–24: 8,220; 25–29: 7,930; 30–34: 7,610; 35–39: 7,150; 40–44: 6,590; 45–49: 6,040; 50–54: 5,370; 55–59: 4,550; 60–64: 3,720; 65–69: 2,960; 70–74: 2,210; 75–79: 1,520; 80+: 1,540 (total weight $W = \\sum w_i = 100{,}000$).\n",
        "\n",
        "The population denominator for each year × age group × sex cell derives from INE census projections. For each stratum defined by year $t$, sex $s$, and age group $i$, the age-specific hospitalization rate was calculated as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{Rate}_{i,t,s} = \\frac{d_{i,t,s}}{N_{i,t,s}} \\times 100{,}000$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $d_{i,t,s}$ denotes the number of hospital discharges and $N_{i,t,s}$ the corresponding mid-year population estimate.\n",
        "\n",
        "The age-standardized rate (ASR) was obtained by weighting these specific rates according to the standard population:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{ASR}_{t,s} = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{17} \\text{Rate}_{i,t,s} \\times w_i}{\\sum_{i=1}^{17} w_i}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $w_i$ is the WHO weight for age group $i$. This formulation removes the confounding effect of differential age structures across regions and time periods, enabling valid comparisons.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Proportional Redistribution of Age Bands\n",
        "\n",
        "Because EGRESOS data from 2010 to 2023 report age in broad 10-year bands (e.g., \"10 to 19\"), while WHO standardization requires 5-year bands, a proportional redistribution procedure was applied. For a given 10-year age band $B$ (e.g., 10–19) comprising two 5-year subgroups $b_1$ (10–14) and $b_2$ (15–19), the hospitalizations were apportioned as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$d_{b_j} = d_B \\times \\frac{N_{b_j}}{N_{b_1} + N_{b_2}}, \\quad j \\in \\{1, 2\\}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $d_B$ is the total discharge count in the 10-year band and $N_{b_j}$ is the population of the $j$-th 5-year subgroup in the corresponding year, sex, and geographic unit. This approach assumes that the age-specific hospitalization rate is approximately uniform within each 10-year band — a standard assumption in demographic redistribution when finer age detail is unavailable. Data from 2024, which are already provided in 5-year bands, were assigned directly without redistribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Sex Ratio and Temporal Trends\n",
        "\n",
        "The male-to-female sex ratio was computed annually as the quotient of the male ASR over the female ASR:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{SR}_t = \\frac{\\text{ASR}_{t,\\text{male}}}{\\text{ASR}_{t,\\text{female}}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Values above 1.0 indicate male predominance, while values below 1.0 indicate female predominance. Temporal trends in ASR were assessed descriptively through time-series visualization and percentage change calculations between the baseline year (2010) and the final year (2024), defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\Delta\\% = \\frac{\\text{ASR}_{2024} - \\text{ASR}_{2010}}{\\text{ASR}_{2010}} \\times 100$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Geospatial Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "### Municipal-Level Rate Estimation\n",
        "\n",
        "ASR values at the municipal (comuna) level were calculated by aggregating hospitalizations and population denominators across the entire 2010–2024 period for each municipality, then applying the same direct standardization procedure described above. The resulting rates were joined with a Chilean municipalities shapefile for cartographic visualization using choropleth maps. Island municipalities (Easter Island, Juan Fernández, and Antarctic territories) were excluded from spatial analysis due to their geographic isolation and absence of contiguous neighbors.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Spatial Weights Matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "Spatial relationships between municipalities were formalized through a Queen contiguity weights matrix $\\mathbf{W}$ [@anselin1988spatial], where two municipalities $i$ and $j$ are considered neighbors ($w_{ij} = 1$) if they share any portion of their boundary (edge or vertex), and $w_{ij} = 0$ otherwise, with $w_{ii} = 0$ by convention. The weights were row-standardized such that $\\sum_j w_{ij} = 1$ for each municipality $i$, ensuring that the spatial lag of a variable represents the weighted average of its neighbors' values.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Global Spatial Autocorrelation — Moran's I\n",
        "\n",
        "Global spatial autocorrelation was assessed using Moran's I statistic [@moran1950notes], defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$I = \\frac{n}{\\sum_{i}\\sum_{j} w_{ij}} \\times \\frac{\\sum_{i}\\sum_{j} w_{ij}(x_i - \\bar{x})(x_j - \\bar{x})}{\\sum_{i}(x_i - \\bar{x})^2}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $n$ is the number of spatial units, $x_i$ is the ASR value for municipality $i$, $\\bar{x}$ is the overall mean, and $w_{ij}$ are the elements of the spatial weights matrix. Moran's I ranges approximately from $-1$ (perfect dispersion) to $+1$ (perfect clustering), with the expected value under the null hypothesis of spatial randomness being $E[I] = -1/(n-1)$. Statistical significance was assessed using the analytical normal approximation, where the $z$-score is computed as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$z = \\frac{I - E[I]}{\\sqrt{\\text{Var}(I)}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and evaluated against the standard normal distribution at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Local Indicators of Spatial Association (LISA)\n",
        "\n",
        "To identify the specific location of spatial clusters and outliers, Local Indicators of Spatial Association [LISA; @anselin1995local] were computed. The local Moran's $I_i$ for each municipality $i$ is defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$I_i = \\frac{(x_i - \\bar{x})}{\\sigma^2_x} \\sum_{j} w_{ij}(x_j - \\bar{x})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\sigma^2_x = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i(x_i - \\bar{x})^2$. Statistical significance of each local statistic was determined through a conditional randomization procedure with 999 permutations, where the value of $x_i$ is held fixed and the remaining $n-1$ values are randomly permuted across spatial locations to generate a reference distribution under the null hypothesis of spatial randomness. Pseudo $p$-values were computed as the proportion of permuted statistics equal to or more extreme than the observed $I_i$, and significance was set at $p < 0.05$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Based on the combination of sign and significance, each municipality was classified into one of five categories: High-High (hot spot: high ASR municipality surrounded by high ASR neighbors), Low-Low (cold spot: low ASR surrounded by low ASR), High-Low (spatial outlier: high ASR surrounded by low ASR), Low-High (spatial outlier: low ASR surrounded by high ASR), or Not Significant.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Diagnostic Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "ICD-10 codes were grouped into 10 disease categories: thyroid disorders (E00-E07), diabetes mellitus (E08-E13), other glucose regulation disorders (E15-E16), disorders of other endocrine glands (E20-E35), intraoperative complications (E36), malnutrition (E40-E46), other nutritional deficiencies (E50-E64), obesity and overweight (E65-E68), metabolic disorders (E70-E88), and post-surgical endocrine complications (E89).\n",
        "\n",
        "## Software\n",
        "\n",
        "All analyses were conducted in Python 3.11. Age standardization and data manipulation were performed using pandas (v2.0+) and NumPy. Geospatial operations used GeoPandas for spatial joins and cartographic rendering, libpysal for the construction of spatial weights matrices, and esda for the computation of global Moran's I and local LISA statistics. Visualization was produced with Matplotlib and Seaborn. The computational document was rendered using Quarto with embedded Python code blocks to ensure full reproducibility.\n",
        "\n",
